explanation:
Tc-99m MDP bone scan detects areas of increased osteoblastic activity, not specifically malignancy. Therefore, many benign conditions can produce false-positive hot spots, especially degenerative joint disease (osteoarthritis).
Degenerative changes commonly cause increased tracer uptake in weight-bearing joints (spine, knees, ribs, pelvis) and may mimic metastasis. Correlation with clinical findings, PSA/ALP levels, and other imaging helps distinguish benign degenerative uptake from true skeletal metastases.
@Neet_pg_bot

Left upper lobe collapse on chest X-ray is suggested by a veil-like opacity over the left lung with elevation of the left hilum and the classic Luftsichel sign (crescent of air adjacent to the aortic arch due to hyperinflated lower lobe).
On lateral view, anterior displacement of the oblique fissure confirms upper lobe collapse. Recognition of lobar collapse patterns helps localize disease and identify underlying causes such as endobronchial obstruction (e.g., TB, tumor, mucus plug).
@Neet_pg_bot

Breast cancer staging uses the TNM system, where T is based on tumor size/local invasion, N describes regional lymph node involvement, and M denotes distant metastasis.
A breast lump measuring 3.6 cm falls into T2 (tumor &gt;2 cm but ≤5 cm) with no skin ulceration or chest wall invasion (so not T4). The presence of multiple hard fixed axillary lymph nodes suggests matted/fixed ipsilateral axillary nodes, corresponding to N2 disease. In addition, axillary lymph nodes on both sides (contralateral axillary nodes) are considered distant metastasis, not regional nodal disease; therefore, this is classified as M1. Hence, the correct staging is T2N2M1.
@Neet_pg_bot

Progressive post-prandial abdominal pain (intestinal angina) beginning 20–30 minutes after meals, lasting 1–2 hours, associated with food fear and significant weight loss in an older patient with major atherosclerotic risk factors (smoking, diabetes, hypertension) is classic for chronic mesenteric ischemia (CMI) due to atherosclerotic stenosis/occlusion of mesenteric arteries (most commonly SMA ± celiac). A faint epigastric bruit further supports vascular disease. Because endoscopy and routine labs are often normal in CMI, diagnosis requires vascular imaging.
The most appropriate confirmatory investigation is CT angiography (CTA) of abdominal vessels, as it provides rapid, high-resolution visualization of the celiac, SMA, and IMA, identifies the site and severity of stenosis, evaluates collateral flow, and simultaneously aids planning for revascularization (endovascular stenting or bypass). Duplex mesenteric Doppler can be used as a screening test but is operator dependent and limited in obese/bowel gas; CTA is the preferred confirmatory test in most patients.
@Neet_pg_bot

Functional MRI (fMRI) is based on Blood Oxygen Level–Dependent (BOLD) contrast, which detects changes in regional cerebral blood flow and oxygenation associated with neuronal activity.
Active brain areas consume more oxygen, leading to local changes in oxyhemoglobin/deoxyhemoglobin ratio that alter magnetic properties and signal intensity on MRI, allowing mapping of functional brain activity without contrast injection.
@Neet_pg_bot

Neisseria gonorrhoeae frequently causes reinfection despite prior antibody formation because it undergoes antigenic variation of its surface pili and outer membrane proteins.
These genetic changes alter antigenic structures, allowing the bacteria to evade previously formed antibodies and preventing the development of long-lasting protective immunity.
@Neet_pg_bot

The median sacral artery is not an anterior branch of the abdominal aorta, but rather a **terminal branch** that arises from the posterior aspect of the aorta at its bifurcation into the common iliac arteries. It descends in the midline anterior to the sacrum and coccyx. This artery is unpaired but is not classified as an anterior branch due to its position and point of origin.
The main unpaired anterior branches of the abdominal aorta include the celiac trunk (T12-L1), superior mesenteric artery (L1), and inferior mesenteric artery (L3), which supply the foregut, midgut, and hindgut respectively.
@Neet_pg_bot

