A patient with fever and right upper quadrant pain with a solitary hypoechoic liver lesion and aspiration yielding sterile brown “anchovy sauce” material is most consistent with an amoebic liver abscess (ALA) due to Entamoeba histolytica. ALA classically occurs in adult males, is often solitary and located in the right lobe, and the aspirate is typically sterile because it contains necrotic hepatocytes rather than pus with bacteria.
Importantly, serology may be negative early in the illness (especially at admission in the first week), so a negative test does not exclude amoebic abscess when the clinical and aspirate features are typical. Therefore, the best next step is empiric anti-amoebic therapy with metronidazole, which is the first-line treatment and usually leads to rapid clinical improvement. Drainage/aspiration is reserved for selected cases (very large abscess, left lobe, risk of rupture, poor response to drugs, or diagnostic uncertainty). Broad-spectrum antibiotics are preferred for pyogenic abscess, which typically yields frank pus and positive cultures. Thus, start metronidazole therapy.
@Neet_pg_bot

