* In adolescents, a very important association to actively suspect is a Müllerian/outflow tract obstruction, which increases risk of endometriosis due to retrograde menstruation.
&gt; **Other Options:**
&gt;
&gt; **Option B. Cervical carcinoma**: Unikely at 14 years and does not explain progressive dysmenorrhea with an adnexal cyst containing internal echoes.
&gt;
&gt; **Option C. Premature ovarian insufficiency:** Would more typically present with oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, low estrogen symptoms, and elevated gonadotropins, not a tender adnexal mass with internal echoes.
&gt;
&gt; **Option D. Polycystic ovarian syndrome**: PCOS presents with oligo-anovulation, hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology.
&gt; It does not typically cause progressive severe dysmenorrhea or a tender endometrioma-like cyst.
