The most appropriate investigation to establish the diagnosis and identify the bleeding source is CT angiography of the hepatic artery, as it can demonstrate contrast extravasation, pseudoaneurysm, or arterio-biliary fistula, and guides definitive management (angiographic embolization). Endoscopy may show blood at the ampulla but does not localize the vascular lesion; MRCP outlines biliary anatomy but does not reliably identify active vascular bleeding; ERCP is primarily therapeutic (clot extraction/stenting) after diagnosis is established.
