Hill’s criteria for disease causation include:
**(1) Strength of association**, where a higher relative risk or odds ratio suggests a stronger likelihood of causality;
**(2) Consistency**, meaning the association is repeatedly observed in different studies, populations, and settings;
**(3) Specificity**, in which a single exposure leads to a specific disease (though this is the weakest criterion and lack of specificity does not exclude causation);
**(4) Temporality**, the most important and mandatory criterion, which states that the exposure must precede the disease;
**(5) Biological gradient (dose–response relationship)**, where increasing exposure results in increasing disease risk;
**(6) Biological plausibility**, indicating that the association is biologically reasonable based on existing knowledge;
**(7) Coherence**, whereby the observed association does not contradict known facts about the natural history and biology of the disease;
**(8) Experiment**, in which removal or reduction of the exposure leads to a decrease in disease occurrence; and
**(9) Analogy**, where similar exposures are known to cause similar effects.
**Sensitivity** is a measure of a **diagnostic test’s ability to detect disease** and is **not included** among the Bradford Hill causal criteria.
